  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify that this is an isosceles triangle with two sides of length 5 units and a base of 6 units.\n\nStep 2: Use the formula for the area of a triangle, \\(A = \\frac{1}{2}bh\\), where \\(b\\) is the base and \\(h\\) is the height.\n\nStep 3: To find the height, drop a perpendicular from the vertex opposite the base to the midpoint of the base, creating two right triangles. Each right triangle will have a hypotenuse of 5 units (the equal side of the isosceles triangle) and one leg of 3 units (half of the base).\n\nStep 4: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to one of the right triangles: \\(5^2 = 3^2 + h^2\\).\n\nStep 5: Solve for \\(h\\): \\(25 = 9 + h^2\\), thus \\(h^2 = 16\\), and \\(h = 4\\).\n\nStep 6: Substitute \\(b = 6\\) and \\(h = 4\\) into the area formula: \\(A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times 4\\).\n\nStep 7: Calculate the area: \\(A = 12\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{12}<|im_end|>",
